WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.560
When Joe Biden and Democrats were able to&nbsp;
pass their one point nine trillion dollar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:10.240
covid relief bill, there was some news&nbsp;
that tax reform might be Joe Biden's next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:17.280
big legislative goal. And we talked last week&nbsp;
about what is expected to be in that tax reform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:22.400
plan. We talked with Bill Share about the&nbsp;
political feasibility, remembering that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:28.560
there are many Democrats who don't necessarily&nbsp;
support all aspects of what Joe Biden wants to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.720
from the standpoint of tax reform. But now it&nbsp;
looks like it may not actually be tax reform that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:39.520
Joe Biden and his administration tackle next.
There's a report from the Associated Press&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:46.400
that it may be a very large a three trillion&nbsp;
dollar, I guess we're calling it an infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:54.800
package, but it's really like an infrastructure&nbsp;
schools and families package. And Joe Biden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:01.920
reportedly Monday met with Senate Democrats to&nbsp;
start laying the groundwork for what would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:08.800
a spending package that would deal with&nbsp;
many aspects of American infrastructure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:17.200
that are sadly outdated. We've needed like real.&nbsp;
I don't mean you drive by and that stretch of road&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.760
near your house is under construction for two&nbsp;
years and it's never really clear what they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:26.560
doing, but eventually it gets done. And now&nbsp;
I'm not talking about that. I'm talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:33.600
major repair and replacement of significant&nbsp;
American infrastructure. We're talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:40.720
new development of roads. We're talking&nbsp;
about a refreshing hospital infrastructure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:47.200
green energy systems, you know, a lot of these&nbsp;
different things. And reportedly, this is exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:54.560
what Joe Biden is looking at next. Three trillion&nbsp;
dollars in total. And it will include money for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:02.240
schools. It will include money for families.&nbsp;
And it would be divided up. Could be a third,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.880
a third, a third. Again, this is all early, but&nbsp;
it looks like it could be about a trillion dollars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:14.080
for roads, bridges, rail lines, electrical vehicle&nbsp;
charging stations and a cellular network. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:20.960
idea would be start pushing towards clean energy,&nbsp;
but do it in an in an economically competitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:27.680
way, which is what we've been saying for all&nbsp;
all this time. The idea is not necessarily to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:33.440
take things out of the market, but it's let's have&nbsp;
fair markets where we're not subsidizing the wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:39.360
things. The second piece of it, which is&nbsp;
the school piece, would include investments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:47.040
in workers by providing free community college,&nbsp;
universal pre-K prekindergarten and paid family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:51.680
leave. Another aspect that we've been talking&nbsp;
about for a while, which many on the right see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:58.320
as, quote, handouts. Remember, it's our tax&nbsp;
money that's then being being used for programs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:04.000
to call them handouts is wrong fundamentally. But&nbsp;
it also ignores that when you're doing something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.800
that allows people to be more economically&nbsp;
productive, it should fit under the umbrella&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:14.880
of what the so-called free enterprise people would&nbsp;
also want. Now, that three trillion dollar number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:22.960
is obviously a number that is going to lead&nbsp;
to the predictable and and regular refrains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:28.240
from the right. And I think that hopefully&nbsp;
Democrats will realize we're not going to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:33.040
votes from Republicans anyway. So let's not care&nbsp;
about the fact that they're going to talk about we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:36.640
don't have the money, where's the money going to&nbsp;
come from, et cetera, et cetera. They're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:41.600
do that regardless. They would do it if it was two&nbsp;
trillion. They did it with 800 billion when it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:47.760
Barack Obama's spending program. They're going&nbsp;
to do it regardless. So hopefully that's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:53.440
something that is going to be affecting the way&nbsp;
that Democrats go about this. And fundamentally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:59.920
as I said, when when Donald Trump lost and&nbsp;
remember, we knew that on November 8th of 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:06.480
we didn't have to wait until January 20th to&nbsp;
know that. I said these two years will be a test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:14.080
in two ways. One, can Republicans obstruct&nbsp;
everything and still get themselves reelected?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:18.640
The answer may be yes. And we'll actually talk&nbsp;
later today about how even though every Republican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:26.240
voted against covid stimulus a few weeks ago, one&nbsp;
third of Republicans, Republican voters think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:31.440
their Republican elected officials voted for&nbsp;
covid stimulus. So they may be able to obstruct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:35.440
and not get punished by their voters because their&nbsp;
voters are so clueless. That's number one. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:41.280
number two, it's a test of what can you get done&nbsp;
when you have a two year period with the White&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:48.560
House, the House and the Senate? We've seen these&nbsp;
periods before. And we the infrastructure spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:54.720
needs to happen. It's been necessary for decades.&nbsp;
We are able to count on zero Republican senators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:59.840
to vote for it. Their efforts will be obstruction&nbsp;
because ultimately. They are cult members,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:04.560
they turned over the party to Donald&nbsp;
Trump when it became clear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:09.840
Trump had the momentum and even if privately&nbsp;
they were opposed to what Trump was doing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:15.120
they stood aside, I guess, because they wanted to&nbsp;
get their judges done or whatever the case may be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:20.640
And I know that one of the talking points&nbsp;
they may come up with and and unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:26.080
this one resonates even with some in my audience,&nbsp;
will be three trillion dollars for all of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:32.400
different stuff. Why can't we just separate it out&nbsp;
and do individual bills up and down votes on each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:38.400
of these elements? And then it would be clearer&nbsp;
what people are in favor of or against, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:42.880
you can always have Republicans saying, well,&nbsp;
I'm not against doing some of this stuff, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:47.680
we can't do all of it because of item number one&nbsp;
and item number seven and twenty. Those are pork&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:53.840
or those are bad or whatever the case may be. What&nbsp;
you have to remember is that bills typically need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.840 --> 00:06:01.040
60 votes, but you can get around that through the&nbsp;
budget reconciliation process. But you're limited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:08.240
in how many times you can use that process. Once&nbsp;
we know and we know that Republicans will not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:13.920
actually negotiate in good faith, they'll find if&nbsp;
you change how you do the bill, they'll find you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:20.000
new ways to oppose it. What you have to understand&nbsp;
is that reconciliation and doing this with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:26.800
50 votes plus Comilla is probably the only way you&nbsp;
get it done. If you separate this out into four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:33.760
or eight or 12 different bills, Republicans will&nbsp;
then just find ways to delay each individual bill.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:38.560
They might use different justifications or&nbsp;
the same one. The only way you really have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:44.400
a shot at getting this done is by doing it&nbsp;
all together in what's called an omnibus bill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:49.920
and passing it through budget reconciliation.&nbsp;
So ultimately, they would say Republicans, oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:54.000
break it up and then we'll do individual votes on&nbsp;
the merits of each one of these. Then they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:58.560
just obstruct and use delay tactics to make sure&nbsp;
that as few of those things actually get a vote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:04.320
in the first place and then they would still vote&nbsp;
against it in the end. So I do think it's probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:09.920
more politically feasible right now for Biden&nbsp;
to do this than the tax stuff. That's that's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:16.320
political feasibility calculation. I think either&nbsp;
way, he's going to be doing it with literally zero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:21.120
Republican support. If you think some&nbsp;
Republicans might vote for such a spending bill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:26.720
infrastructure bill, let me know. And specifically&nbsp;
let me know who, because I just don't see it

